5. bc6h_enc, by Alexander Samusev (https://github.com/0xc0de/bc6h_enc), which is the BC6H
   encoder of DirectXTex, by Microsoft (https://github.com/microsoft/DirectXTex,
   BC6HBC7.cpp), made into one file: the encoder of its bc6h_enc.h, with its tables
   (src/bc6h_enc.rs, src/bc6h_enc_tables.rs).

    Copyright (c) 2022 Alexander Samusev
    Copyright (c) 2011-2022 Microsoft Corp

   Both are under the MIT licence; those notices are kept in LICENSE-bc6h_enc.txt. The file
   is changed here in that it is translated from C++ to Rust, and its decoder is left out.
   What it computes is kept: the same blocks as the original built with
   BC6H_SSE_INTRINSICS.

6. miniz, by Rich Geldreich and others (https://github.com/richgel999/miniz): its
   compressor (miniz_tdef.c), its decompressor (miniz_tinfl.c) and the two checksums of
   miniz.c (src/miniz).

    Copyright 2013-2014 RAD Game Tools and Valve Software
    Copyright 2010-2014 Rich Geldreich and Tenacious Software LLC

   Under the MIT licence; that notice is kept in LICENSE-miniz.txt. The files are changed
   here in that they are translated from C to Rust: the compressor writes through its own
   buffer only, and the decompressor is the original's one function as it runs when given
   all its input and all its room at once. What they compute is kept: the same compressed
   bytes as the original as it builds by default, and the same streams taken and refused.

7. stb, by Sean Barrett and others (https://github.com/nothings/stb): the PNG, BMP and JPEG
   writers of its stb_image_write.h, with the zlib compressor the PNG writer uses
   (src/stb_image_write.rs, src/stb_image_write_tables.rs), and every reader of its
   stb_image.h (PNG, BMP, JPEG, TGA, PSD, GIF, PNM and Radiance HDR), with the zlib decoder
   the PNG reader uses (src/stb_image).
   The JPEG writer is based on Jon Olick's jo_jpeg.cpp, which is in the public domain.

    Copyright (c) 2017 Sean Barrett

   Those files are offered by their author under the MIT licence or as public domain
   (unlicense.org), whichever the user prefers; that notice is kept in LICENSE-stb.txt. They
   are changed here in that they are translated from C to Rust: the settings the originals
   keep in globals are a value handed to each call, the writers give back the bytes of the
   file and the readers take them, a refused file's reason comes back with the refusal, and
   the writers of other formats are left out. What they compute is kept: the
   same files as stb_image_write.h built without fused multiply-add, and the same pixels and
   refusals as stb_image.h built with STBI_NO_SIMD, with memory that starts as zeros and
   with integer overflow that wraps.

8. Zstandard, by Meta Platforms, Inc. and affiliates (https://github.com/facebook/zstd),
   v1.5.7: the reference decoder of its doc/educational_decoder/zstd_decompress.c
   (src/zstd/zstd_decompress.rs), and of its compressor, in lib/compress and lib/common, the
   parts that one call of ZSTD_compress uses at every level, with all nine strategies:
   bitstream.h, fse.h and fse_compress.c, hist.c, huf_compress.c, zstd_compress_literals.c,
   zstd_compress_sequences.c, zstd_fast.c, zstd_double_fast.c, zstd_lazy.c, zstd_opt.c,
   zstd_ldm.c, zstd_preSplit.c, clevels.h,
   zstd_compress_internal.h and zstd_compress.c (the files of the same names in src/zstd,
   with common.rs for what they share).

    Copyright (c) Meta Platforms, Inc. and affiliates. All rights reserved.

   Offered by its authors under a BSD licence or the GNU General Public License, version 2,
   whichever the user prefers; it is taken here under the BSD licence, which is kept in
   LICENSE-zstd.txt. The files are changed here in that they are translated from C to Rust.
   Where the decoder stops the program with a message, the port gives the message back, and
   its output grows as it is written; what it computes is kept: the same bytes from every
   frame, and the same message for a frame it refuses. Of the compressor, what a frame made
   in one call from a source that is all there does not use is left out (dictionaries,
   checksums, streaming, threads), places
   in the source stand for pointers, and the work areas the original carves out of one block
   of memory are fields and local arrays; what it computes is kept: the same frame, byte for
   byte, as ZSTD_compress gives for a source and a level.

9. tinyexr, by Syoyo Fujita and many contributors (https://github.com/syoyo/tinyexr),
   v3.4.0: of its tinyexr.h, the reading and the writing of files of one part and of many,
   and the reading of deep files (src/tinyexr), with the compressors and decompressors it has
   from OpenEXR (RLE, PIZ, B44) and its own (ZIP by way of miniz, PXR24, ZFP by way of the
   zfp library).

    Copyright (c) 2014 - 2021, Syoyo Fujita and many contributors. All rights reserved.
    Copyright (c) 2002, Industrial Light & Magic, a division of Lucas Digital Ltd. LLC.
    All rights reserved.

   Both under BSD 3-clause licences, kept in LICENSE-tinyexr.txt. The file is changed here
   in that it is translated from C++ to Rust: a file comes in and goes out as its bytes, a
   refusal's code and message come back with it, the values of a channel are a vector of
   their type where the original has bytes, and memory starts as zeros. The spectral
   helpers and threads are left out. What it computes is kept: the
   same files, the same headers, the same values and the same refusals as tinyexr.h built
   without threads and with GNU libstdc++ (the lengths of PIZ's Huffman codes depend on how
   the C++ library orders a heap). It is changed in four ways. Where the original stops the
   program or reads past the end of a damaged file (a tile size of 0, a count of chunks
   beyond what memory holds, a compressed tile placed past the image's edge, a block that is
   not compressed and is shorter than its lines), the port refuses the file. Where the
   original's PIZ writer writes past its buffer, the port has room. What the writer is
   handed is checked where the original trusts it. And PXR24, B44 and B44A blocks that do
   not come out smaller than their pixels are written as the pixels, and blocks as long as
   their pixels read as them, as the format has it; the functions named `..._as_tinyexr` do
   as the original does. Where its readers of multi-part and deep files read past the end
   of a damaged file, the port makes the reads with zeros and with ones there, and refuses
   the file where the two differ. And DWAA and DWAB, which the original does not have, are
   read and written by the port of OpenEXR's (11, below).

10. zfp, by Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC (https://github.com/LLNL/zfp),
    v1.0.1: of its library, the bit stream, and the coding of two-dimensional blocks of
    floats at a fixed rate, precision or accuracy (in src/tinyexr/zfp.rs), as tinyexr uses
    them.

    Copyright (c) 2014-2023, Lawrence Livermore National Security, LLC. All rights reserved.

   Under the BSD 3-clause licence, kept with its notice in LICENSE-zfp.txt. The files are
   changed here in that they are translated from C to Rust, and what tinyexr does not use
   (other dimensions and types, the reversible mode, the other execution policies) is left
   out. What they compute is kept: the same bits.

11. OpenEXR, by the Contributors to the OpenEXR Project
    (https://github.com/AcademySoftwareFoundation/openexr), v3.5.4: of its OpenEXRCore, the
    DWAA and DWAB compressor and decompressor (internal_dwa.c and the headers
    internal_dwa_*.h, with the lookup tables internal_dwa_table_init.c makes) and the
    Huffman coder of internal_huf.c (src/dwa), and the RLE and the byte order of ZIP that
    DWA uses (internal_rle.c, internal_zip.c).

    Copyright (c) Contributors to the OpenEXR Project. All rights reserved.
    Copyright (c) DreamWorks Animation LLC and Contributors of the OpenEXR Project.

   Under the BSD 3-clause licence, kept in LICENSE-openexr.txt. The files are changed here in
   that they are translated from C to Rust: the code OpenEXRCore picks on an x86-64 machine
   with AVX and F16C (its AVX inverse DCT and its F16C conversions, written in assembly) is
   written as the arithmetic it does, and so runs on every machine; its lookup tables are
   kept as glibc's powf and logf make them; and a block comes in and goes out as its bytes.
   What it computes is kept: the same blocks, the same pixels and the same refusals as
   OpenEXRCore on such a machine. Where a damaged block would have it read past its buffer of
   AC coefficients, the port refuses the block.

12. libdeflate, by Eric Biggers and others (https://github.com/ebiggers/libdeflate), v1.26,
    as OpenEXR 3.5 comes with it: its deflate_compress.c (levels 0 and 2 to 9, with
    hc_matchfinder.h), deflate_decompress.c, zlib_compress.c, zlib_decompress.c and
    adler32.c (src/libdeflate).

    Copyright 2016 Eric Biggers
    Copyright 2024 Google LLC

   Under the MIT licence, kept in LICENSE-libdeflate.txt. The files are changed here in that
   they are translated from C to Rust: the decompressor decodes as the original's careful
   loop does, a codeword at a time, and the compressor's buffers are its own. What they
   compute is kept: the same streams, the same bytes from them, and the same refusals.

What is around the ports is the crate's own: mip levels, the HDR image and the reading and
writing of picture files by the ports of stb and of tinyexr (in src/image.rs),
float_to_half (in src/bc6h.rs), src/bc2.rs (BC2, its colours packed and unpacked by the
port of rgbcx), BC2 in src/rdo_bc_encoder.rs, src/texture.rs,
src/dds.rs (its writer after bc7enc_rdo's save_dds), src/ktx.rs and src/parallel.rs. The
data format descriptor src/ktx.rs writes into a .ktx2 file follows the one the Khronos
Group's KTX-Software writes (createDFDCompressed in its dfdutils, Apache 2.0).

The port was made in 2026 for the Noolith project.
